<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The window theme on API 35 and up (D-05f, ux-spec §6.2; tech-lead defect ruling §1, B-1).
      The app targets API 36, so from API 35 the window is edge to edge whatever the theme says, and
      the platform draws its own contrast scrim behind the three-button navigation bar. Which scrim,
      light under dark buttons or dark under light ones, is windowLightNavigationBar's answer, but
      the platform honours it only while the theme names a navigation-bar colour. A theme that
      names none leaves the bar to the window background instead, and Material.Light's background
      is near-white in both modes, so the platform forced a light bar on dark mode too (B-1).

      So this style names the bar's colour: transparent, the platform's own value and no colour of
      the app's. From API 35 the platform keeps the bar transparent whatever is named, so nothing a
      player sees changes; naming it only turns the forcing off and lets windowLightNavigationBar
      decide. Do not remove the item as unused or deprecated: without it dark mode gets the light
      bar back.

      Below API 35 res/values/themes.xml governs: the window is not edge to edge there, and the
      platform's opaque bar is left as it is (B-1b). This file restates the WHOLE style, the
      status-bar item included, because styles do not merge across qualifiers.
      WindowThemeApi35FilesTest pins the three items.
      (2026-11-01, D-05g: below API 35 the window is now edge to edge too, through MainActivity's
      enableEdgeToEdge(), tech design 3.8 (e), which sets the bars there at run time; the items in
      both theme pairs are unchanged.)
    -->
    <style name="Theme.PegSolitaire" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>
</resources>
